<sec:concrete>
== Monads and Monad Transformers
<monads-and-monad-transformers>
As previously stated, every strong monad over a CCC, by virtue of providing a model of higher-order effectful programming, #emph[also] provides a model of first-order effectful programming, i.e., (acyclic) SSA. In particular, the Kleisli category $sans(C)_(sans(T))$ of every such strong monad $sans(T)$ is a premonoidal category and induces a Freyd category with pure morphisms given by $ ( sans(C)_(sans(T)) )_tack.t ( A   B ) = { f ; eta_B : A arrow.r sans(T) B divides f : A arrow.r B } subset.eq sans(C) ( A   sans(T) B ) = sans(C)_(sans(T)) ( A   B ) $ It follows that every strong Elgot monad over a CCC with all coproducts is an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model (since, in particular, every CCC with coproducts is distributive). This allows us to very quickly amass a large collection of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models from the literature on strong Elgot monads (see e.g. @goncharov-18-guarded-traced, @goncharov-21-uniform-elgot).

Probably the simplest example of an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model is given by the #emph[option monad] $sans("Option") #h(0em) A = A + upright(bold(1))$ #footnote[In a constructive setting, we may equivalently use the partial values monad $sans("Partial") #h(0em) A := Sigma phi.alt : sans("Prop") . phi.alt arrow.r A$.], with fixpoint operation $ f^dagger = sans("some") #h(0em) b quad upright("if") quad exists n   f^n #h(0em) a = sans("some") #h(0em) ( iota_l #h(0em) b ) #h(2em) f^dagger = sans("none") quad upright("otherwise") $ where $ f^0 #h(0em) a = sans("some") #h(0em) ( iota_r #h(0em) a ) #h(2em) f^(n + 1) #h(0em) a = sans("bind") #h(0em) ( f^n #h(0em) a ) #h(0em) \[ ( sans("pure") ; iota_l )   f \] $ This model allows us to interpret potentially divergent but otherwise purely functional programs. Another very important $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model is generated by the #emph[powerset monad] $cal(P)$ over $sans("Set")$, which has fixpoint operation $ f^dagger #h(0em) a = union.big_n { b divides iota_l #h(0em) b in f^n #h(0em) a } $ We can use this monad to interpret #emph[nondeterministic] potentially divergent functional programs. We can further expand our repertoire of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models by considering #emph[monad transformers], many of which preserve Elgot-ness. For example, if $sans(T)$ is a strong Elgot over $sans("Set")$,

- For all types $R$, the #emph[reader transformer] $sans("ReaderT") #h(0em) R #h(0em) sans(T) #h(0em) A = R arrow.r sans(T) #h(0em) A$ is strong Elgot with monad operations $ eta #h(0em) a = lambda - . eta_(sans(T)) #h(0em) a #h(2em) sans("bind") #h(0em) a #h(0em) f = lambda r . sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( a #h(0em) r ) #h(0em) ( lambda a . f #h(0em) a #h(0em) r ) $ and fixpoint $ f^dagger = lambda r . ( lambda a . f #h(0em) a #h(0em) r )^(dagger_(sans(T))) $

- For all monoids $W$, the #emph[writer transformer] $sans("WriterT") #h(0em) W #h(0em) sans(T) #h(0em) A = sans(T) #h(0em) ( A times W )$ is strong Elgot with monad operations $ eta #h(0em) a = eta_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( a   1 ) #h(2em) sans("bind") #h(0em) a #h(0em) f = sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) a #h(0em) ( lambda ( a   w ) . sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( f #h(0em) a ) #h(0em) ( lambda ( b   w' ) . ( b   w dot.op w' ) ) ) $ and fixpoint $ f^dagger #h(0em) a = ( lambda ( a   w ) . sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( f #h(0em) a ) #h(0em) ( lambda ( b   w' ) . eta_(sans(T)) ( \[ ( lambda c . thin ( c   w dot.op w' ) )   ( lambda c . thin ( c   w dot.op w' ) ) \] #h(0em) b ) ) )^(dagger_(sans(T))) ( a   1 ) $

- For all types $S$, the #emph[state transformer] $sans("StateT") #h(0em) S #h(0em) sans(T) #h(0em) A = S arrow.r sans(T) ( A times S )$ is strong Elgot with monad operations $ eta #h(0em) a = lambda s . eta_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( a   s ) #h(2em) sans("bind") #h(0em) a #h(0em) f = lambda s . sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( a #h(0em) s ) #h(0em) ( lambda ( a   s' ) . f #h(0em) a #h(0em) s' ) $ and fixpoint $ f^dagger = lambda s . ( lambda ( a   s' ) . sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) ( f #h(0em) a #h(0em) s' ) #h(0em) ( lambda ( b   s' ) . eta_(sans(T)) ( \[ ( lambda c . thin ( c   s' ) )   ( lambda c . ( c   s' ) ) \] #h(0em) b ) ) )^(dagger_(sans(T))) $

One other important source of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models is via #emph[Elgot submonads] of monads on $sans("Set")$, which we define as follows:

#block[
A monad $( sans(S)   eta'   mu' )$ is a submonad of $( sans(T)   eta   mu )$ if $eta = eta'$, $forall A   sans(S) #h(0em) A subset.eq sans(T) #h(0em) A$, and, on the set $sans(S) #h(0em) ( sans(S) #h(0em) A ) subset.eq sans(T) #h(0em) ( sans(T) #h(0em) A )$, $mu = mu'$. We say $sans(S)$ is #emph[strong Elgot] if $sans(T)$ is strong Elgot and, for all $f in A arrow.r sans(S) #h(0em) ( A + B )$, $f^dagger in A arrow.r sans(S) #h(0em) B$ (i.e. the fixpoint operation sends the Kleisli category of $sans(S)$ to itself).

]
One useful property this definition has is that the intersection of arbitrarily many (Elgot) submonads forms an (Elgot) submonad, i.e., the (Elgot) submonads form a complete lattice.

== Trace Models
<trace-models>
A particularly important class of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models are what we will call #emph[trace models], which can be viewed as programs which either diverge or produce a result, while also producing (potentially nondeterministic, potentially infinite) traces of events $epsilon.alt in E$.

#block[
A #emph[stream action] $( M   I   dot.op   Sigma )$ is a monoid actions $( M   I   dot.op )$ of #emph[finite effects] $M$ acting on #emph[divergent effects] $I$, along with a function $Sigma : M^omega arrow.r I$ mapping infinite streams of $M$ to an element of $I$ satisfying $Sigma sigma = sigma_0 dot.op Sigma_i sigma_(i + 1)$, where $Sigma_i sigma_i = Sigma ( lambda i . sigma_i )$.

]
The most important example of a stream action is the #emph[free] stream action over a set $E$, called the set of #emph[events], where:

- $M = E^(\*)$ is the free monoid over a set of “events" $epsilon.alt in E$, i.e., the monoid of finite lists of events

- $I = E^(lt.eq omega)$ is the set of #emph[potentially] infinite streams of events $epsilon.alt in E$

- $m dot.op i$ is defined in the obvious manner, i.e. by prepending the (finite) list $m$ to the (potentially infinite) stream $i$

- $Sigma #h(0em) sigma$ is defined as the supremum $union.sq.big_i t_i$, where $t lt.eq t'$ iff $t$ is a prefix of $t'$, $t_0 = tack.t$ (the empty stream), and $t_(i + 1) = t_i dot.op sigma_i$

#block[
Given a stream action $( M   I   dot.op   Sigma )$, we can define the #emph[trace monad transformer] over $sans("Set")$ as follows: $sans("TraceT") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) sans(T) #h(0em) A = sans(T) #h(0em) ( A times M + I )$ with monad operations $ eta = ( lambda a . iota_l ( a   0 ) ) ; eta_(sans(T)) #h(2em) sans("bind") #h(0em) a #h(0em) f = sans("bind")_(sans(T)) #h(0em) a #h(0em) \[ lambda ( a   m ) . m dot.op f a   lambda i . eta_(sans(T)) ( iota_r #h(0em) i ) \] $ This yields a family of useful Elgot monads, including

- $sans("Traces") ? #h(0em) Sigma = sans("TraceT") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) cal(P)$, with the fixpoint of $f : A arrow.r B + A$ given by $ f^dagger #h(0em) a & = f_oo^dagger #h(0em) a union union.big_n f_n^dagger #h(0em) a #h(2em) #h(2em) upright("where")\
  f_n^dagger #h(0em) a & = { iota_l #h(0em) ( b   m ) divides iota_l ( iota_l #h(0em) b   m ) in \[ sans("id")_B   f \]^n ( iota_r #h(0em) a ) } union { iota_r #h(0em) i divides iota_r #h(0em) i in \[ sans("id")_B   f \]^n ( iota_r #h(0em) a ) }\
  f^oo #h(0em) a & = { iota_r Sigma sigma divides exists alpha : A^omega   alpha_0 = a and forall i   iota_l #h(0em) ( iota_r #h(0em) alpha_(i + 1)   sigma_i ) in f #h(0em) alpha_i } $ We say a morphism $f : A arrow.r sans("Traces") ? #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) B$ is #emph[total] if all $f #h(0em) a$ are nonempty, and #emph[deterministic] if all $f #h(0em) a$ are subsingletons.

- $sans("Traces") #h(0em) Sigma = sans("TraceT") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) cal(P)^(+)$, which can be viewed as an Elgot submonad of $sans("Traces") ? #h(0em) Sigma$ since the fixpoint of a total function is total

- $sans("Trace") ? #h(0em) Sigma = sans("TraceT") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) sans("Option")$, which can be viewed as an Elgot submonad of $sans("Traces") ? #h(0em) Sigma$ since the fixpoint of a deterministic function is deterministic

- $sans("Trace") #h(0em) Sigma = sans("TraceT") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) sans("Id")$, which can be viewed as an Elgot submonad of $sans("Traces") ? #h(0em) Sigma$, and in particular the intersection $sans("Trace") ? #h(0em) Sigma inter sans("Traces") #h(0em) Sigma$.

]
As a simple, concrete example of this type of model this construction lets us define, consider the #emph[nondeterministic printing monad], which is simply defined as $sans("Print") #h(0em) A equiv sans("Traces") #h(0em) Sigma$, where $Sigma : ( sans("byte")^(\*) )^omega arrow.r sans("byte")^(lt.eq omega)$ denotes concatenation of bytestrings into a (potentially infinite) bytestream. This gives us us an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model supporting effectful instructions $sans("print") : sans("byte")^(\*) arrow.r upright(bold(1))$ and $sans("nondet") : upright(bold(1)) arrow.r A$ (for $A$ nonempty), with semantics $⟦ sans("print") ⟧ = lambda b . { iota_0 ( ( )   b ) }$, $⟦ sans("nondet") ⟧ = lambda ( ) . { iota_0 ( a   \[ \] ) divides a in A }$.

We can make things a little more interesting by adding a heap to the mix, defining our monad $sans("Comp") = sans("StateT") #h(0em) sans("Heap") #h(0em) sans("Print")$, where $sans("Heap") = bb(N) harpoon.rt bb(N)$ is simply a partial function with finite support. The Kleisli category of this monad is also a Freyd category and inherits an Elgot structure from that on $sans("Set")_(sans("Print"))$; we therefore have an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model supporting the instructions $sans("set") : bb(N) times bb(N) arrow.r upright(bold(1))$, $sans("get") : bb(N) arrow.r bb(N)$, $sans("alloc") : bb(N) arrow.r bb(N) )$, and $sans("free") : bb(N) arrow.r upright(bold(1))$. We can assign semantics to $sans("set")$ in the standard fashion, with $⟦ sans("set") ⟧ = lambda ( p   v ) #h(0em) h . { iota_0 ( ( )   \[ p mapsto v \] h   \[ \] ) }$. $sans("get")$ is a bit more tricky, since it is unclear what to do when we try to access uninitialized memory: one option is simply to return an arbitrary value, with $⟦ sans("get") ⟧ = lambda p #h(0em) h . { iota_0 ( v   h   \[ \] ) divides v = h #h(0em) p or p in.not h }$. Finally, $⟦ sans("alloc") ⟧ = lambda v #h(0em) h . { iota_0 #h(0em) ( p   h'   \[ \] ) divides h' = h union.sq p mapsto v }$ simply fills a random empty heap cell with the provided value, returning a pointer to the cell, while $⟦ sans("free") ⟧ = lambda p #h(0em) h . { iota_0 #h(0em) ( ( )   h \\ p   \[ \] ) }$.

As we can see, the trace monad is a very powerful tool for quickly constructing families of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models, at least for relatively simple side-effects. The resulting family of models is somewhat similar to #emph[interaction trees], which were introduced by #cite(<xia-20-itrees>, form: "prose") to give convenient denotational semantics to imperative programs: these also form an Elgot monad. Because they form an Elgot monad, they immediately form a model of $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$. Similar techniques can be used to tackle much more complex side-effects; to demonstrate this, we will show how the model of TSO weak given in #cite(<sparky>, form: "prose") can be adapted to our framework.

== TSO weak memory
<ssec:tso>
Rather than a sequential trace consisting of a stream of events (here, #emph[actions]) $a_1   a_2   a_3   . . .$, #cite(<sparky>, form: "prose") model concurrency by considering a #emph[partially ordered multiset], or #emph[pomset], of actions. The incomparable actions in our pomset are then precisely those which are executed in parallel. More formally, we define:

#block[
A #emph[pomset] $alpha$ over a set of actions $cal(A)$ with a distinguished null action "#emph[tick]" $delta in cal(A)$ is a #emph[nonempty] partially-ordered #emph[carrier set] $P$ such that every $p in P$ has finitely many predecessors equipped with a mapping $alpha : P arrow.r cal(A)$. A pomset is #emph[finite] if its carrier set is. We will quotient pomsets over the removal of arbitrarily many copies of $delta$ as long as infinite sets are only equated to other infinite pomsets. We will write unordered pomsets using multiset notation, e.g. ${ a   a   b }$, and linearly ordered pomsets using list notation, e.g. $\[ a   a   b \]$.

]
(Finite) pomsets form a monoid under sequential composition $alpha ; beta$, which is defined by the function $\[ alpha   beta \] : sans("trim") ( P + Q ) arrow.r cal(A)$, where $P + Q$ is given the lexicographic ordering and $sans("trim") ( R )$ removes all elements of $R$ with infinitely many predecessors. They also form a monoid under parallel composition, defining $alpha bar.v bar.v beta = \[ alpha   beta \] : P + Q arrow.r cal(A)$ where $P + Q$ is given the standard partial ordering (with elements of $P$ and $Q$ incomparable). Note in both cases the monoidal unit is ${ delta }$, since we only consider nonempty pomsets but allow the removal of finitely many ticks. Given any partially ordered set $N$ and a family of pomsets $alpha_n$ for $n in N$, we can define their #emph[sum] $Sigma_n alpha_n$ to be given by the function $( n   a ) mapsto alpha_n #h(0em) a : sans("trim") ( Sigma_n P_n ) arrow.r cal(A)$, where the dependent product $Sigma_n P_n$ is given the lexicographic order. In particular, choosing $N = bb(N)$ makes $Sigma : sans("Pom")_(sans("fin"))^omega arrow.r sans("Pom")$ into a stream action monoid w.r.t the sequential composition monoid on finite pomsets.

For simplicity, we will consider an infinite set of named locations $x   y   z in sans("Loc")$ which are subject to concurrent modification by all threads via TSO reads, writes, and fences. In particular, we define a #emph[program order pomset] to be a pomset with $cal(A)_(sans("PO")) = cal(A)_w union cal(A)_r union { delta }$, where $cal(A)_r$ consists of #emph[reads] of the form $x = v$ and $cal(A)_w$ consists of #emph[writes] of the form $x := v$ for locations $x$ and values $v in sans("Word")$.

We may now define the #emph[program order monad] $sans("PO") #h(0em) A = sans("Traces") #h(0em) Sigma #h(0em) A$, where $Sigma$ is taken as a stream action on finite program order pomsets. This yields an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ model with support for concurrent read and write operations $sans("read")_x : cal(I)_0^diameter ( upright(bold(1))   sans("Word") )$, $sans("write")_x : cal(I)_0^diameter ( sans("Word")   upright(bold(1)) )$ with semantics $⟦ sans("read")_x ⟧ = R_x^(sans("PO")) = lambda ( ) . { ( v   { x = v } ) bar.v v in sans("Word") }$, $⟦ sans("write")_x ⟧ = W_x^(sans("PO")) = lambda v . { ( ( )   { x := v } ) }$. The semantics of $sans("read")$ in particular give a hint as to how this model works: rather than tracking the state of the heap, we simply #emph[emit] a pomset of the events that would have been generated by a given execution (in the case of a read, a read event $x = v$ whenever the read returns $v$, and in the case of a write, the single write event $x := v$ for a write of $v$), and later post-filter to obtain a set of valid executions. By doing so, our semantics remains compositional, allowing us to reason about each program fragment individually, while still allowing other program fragments to perform concurrent operations affecting our potential executions. On that note, we can define the #emph[parallel execution] of two morphisms as follows: $ f_0 bar.v bar.v f_1 = lambda ( a_0   a_1 ) . & { iota_0 ( ( b_0   b_1 )   alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) divides iota_0 ( b_i   alpha_i ) in f_i #h(0em) a_i }\
 & union { iota_1 ( alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) divides ( iota_0 ( b_0   alpha_0 ) in f_0 #h(0em) a or iota_0 #h(0em) alpha_0 in f_0 #h(0em) a_0 ) and iota_1 alpha_1 in f_1 #h(0em) a_1 }\
 & union { iota_1 ( alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) divides iota_1 #h(0em) alpha_0 in f_0 #h(0em) a_0 and iota_0 ( a_1   alpha_1 ) in f_1 #h(0em) a_1 } : sans("Set")_(sans("PO")) ( A ⊗ A'   B ⊗ B' ) $ It's tempting to try to use this to define a tensor product of morphisms to obtain a monoidal (rather than premonoidal) category, but unfortunately, sliding still fails: $( W_x^(sans("PO")) bar.v bar.v sans("id") ) ; ( sans("id") bar.v bar.v W_y^(sans("PO")) )$ emits the pomset $\[ x := a   y := b \]$, while $( sans("id") bar.v bar.v W_y^(sans("PO")) ) ; ( W_x^(sans("PO")) bar.v bar.v sans("id") )$ emits the pomset $\[ y := b   x := a \]$.

To graduate from sequential consistency to a genuine, if maximally simple, weak memory model, we introduce #emph[load buffering] of write actions. We will implement TSO ordering by buffering all our writes, in which case they are only visible to the local thread. On the other hand, read events will first attempt to read from the buffer, and, if there is no corresponding write in the buffer, will read an arbitrary value, in both cases pushing an event to the global pomset. At any point, we may choose to #emph[flush] some of the buffered writes. We introduce the set $cal(A)_b = { ( overline(x) := v ) }$ of #emph[buffer write actions], where an action $overline(x) := v$ by a thread denotes adding a write $x := v$ to the thread's write buffer. We may then define the set of TSO actions $cal(A)_(sans("TSO")) = cal(A)_(sans("PO")) union cal(A)_b$; a pomset over this set is called a #emph[TSO pomset]. A buffer will be defined to be a list of write actions $sans("Buf") = cal(A)_b^(\*)$, which we will interpret as linear pomsets over $cal(A)_(sans("TSO"))$ ordered by index (with the empty list corresponding to ${ delta }$). In particular, we define the monad $sans("TSO") = sans("StateT") #h(0em) sans("Buf") #h(0em) ( sans("Trace") #h(0em) Sigma )$, where $Sigma$ is taken as a the stream action of finite TSO pomsets on TSO pomsets. We can view $sans("PO")$ as a submonad of this monad.

Given a buffer $sans("Buf")$, we can define the result of reads $\[ dot.op \]_x : sans("Buf") arrow.r sans("Word") union.sq { tack.t }$ from the buffer by induction to be: $( L ; { overline(x) := v } ) \[ x \] = v  $, $( L ; { overline(y) := v } ) \[ x \] = L \[ x \]$, $\[ \] \[ x \] = tack.t$. Note that writes at the #emph[end] of the buffer are prioritized, since later writes overwrite earlier ones!

The semantics of reads and writes can then be given by $ ⟦ sans("read")_x ⟧ = R_x^(sans("TSO")) & = sans("pflush") ; ( lambda ( ) #h(0em) L . { ( v   L   { x = v } ) divides L \[ x \] = v or L \[ x \] = tack.t } ) ; sans("pflush")\
⟦ sans("write")_x ⟧ = W_x^(sans("TSO")) & = sans("pflush") ; ( lambda v #h(0em) L . { ( v   ( L ; { overline(x) := v } )   { x := v } ) } ) ; sans("pflush") $ where buffer flushing is implemented via the morphism $ sans("pflush")_A = lambda a #h(0em) L . { ( a   R   alpha ) bar.v L = alpha ; R } : sans("Set")_(sans("TSO")) ( A   A ) $ (called $sans("split")$ in @sparky). To be able to perform synchronization, we will also need to introduce a $sans("fence") : cal(I)_0^diameter ( upright(bold(1))   upright(bold(1)) )$ instruction, which simply causes all actions before the fence to be observed before any actions after the fence. For $sans("TSO")$, implementing this is as simple as flushing the buffer, i.e., we define $ ⟦ sans("fence") ⟧ = lambda ( ) #h(0em) L . { ( ( )   \[ \]   L ; { delta } ) } $ We can now define the parallel composition of morphisms in a "fork-join" style as follows: we first flush the buffer completely (i.e., taking it and sticking it at the beginning of our pomset), then execute $f$ and $g$ in parallel with separate buffers, #emph[filtering out executions which completely flush the buffer]. Since both threads end up with an empty buffer, the resulting joined buffer is also empty, giving us the following definition: $ f_0 bar.v bar.v f_1 = lambda ( a_0   a_1 ) #h(0em) L . & { iota_0 ( ( b_0   b_1 )   \[ \]   L ; ( alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) ) divides iota_0 ( b_i   \[ \]   alpha_i ) in f_i #h(0em) a_i #h(0em) \[ \] }\
 & union { iota_1 ( alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) divides ( iota_0 ( b_0   \[ \]   alpha_0 ) in f_0 #h(0em) a or iota_0 #h(0em) alpha_0 in f_0 #h(0em) a_0 #h(0em) \[ \] ) and iota_1 alpha_1 in f_1 #h(0em) a_1 #h(0em) \[ \] }\
 & union { iota_1 ( alpha_0 bar.v bar.v alpha_1 ) divides iota_1 #h(0em) alpha_0 in f_0 #h(0em) a_0 #h(0em) \[ \] and iota_0 ( a_1   \[ \]   alpha_1 ) in f_1 #h(0em) a_1 #h(0em) \[ \] } $

One issue with this model is that our category currently has "too many" operations besides the ones we want: for example, it is a perfectly valid morphism to simply add an event into the buffer without any flushing. Of course, one thing we could do is consider the smallest Elgot subcategory generated by the atomic instructions we want to support, such as $sans("read")_x$, $sans("write")_x$, and $sans("fence")$, which can be a perfectly valid approach, but is somewhat unwieldy. Another approach, however, is to think about what "valid" morphisms might look like in general; this also has the benefit of letting us write down specifications that may not be trivially implementable in terms of actual instructions.

There are many potential properties we might want "valid" morphisms to have; in general, the less valid morphisms we admit, the more equations we have to work with when reasoning. One simple property we might consider is that a "valid" morphism must have at least one execution in which the buffer is completely flushed, regardless of initial state. This is a perfectly reasonable property to impose, since it ensures that we do not encounter degenerate cases where, for example, the parallel composition of two processes has an empty trace set even though both processes have a nonempty trace set (since neither empties the buffer). We might even want to go a little bit further and, representing the fact that a buffer flush can happen at any time between instructions, require that a nondeterministic buffer flush before or after an instruction does not change its semantics, i.e., that $sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B = f$. Unfortunately, this does #emph[not] hold for the identity, since $ sans("pflush") ; sans("id") ; sans("pflush") = sans("pflush") ; sans("pflush") = sans("pflush") eq.not sans("id") $ so this would not give us a subcategory. While one possible solution is to simply add the identity back in (and this would give us a valid subcategory), we have the issue that, for example, morphisms like the associator and unitor would still be excluded. Instead, we can take into account the fact that all $sans("pflush")_A$ are idempotent and define a new category $sans("PTSO")$ with objects sets and morphisms of the form $ sans("PTSO") ( A   B ) = { sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B divides f in sans("Set")_(sans("TSO")) ( A   B ) } $ In this category, $sans("pflush")_A$ is the identity on $A$, since $ sans("pflush")_A ; sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B & = sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B\
sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B ; sans("pflush")_B & = sans("pflush")_A ; f ; sans("pflush")_B $ This is especially useful since it frees us from needing to remember to sprinkle $sans("pflush")$ everywhere when specifying things (and, of course, when that level of control is desired, we can specify in $sans("Set")_(sans("TSO"))$ and then lift to $sans("PTSO")$).

It turns out that this is a general construction we can do, which gives us another useful way to build up $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ models that can often be quite difficult to express as simply monads. In particular, we have the following definition

#block[
Let $cal(C)$ be a category and $d$ be a family of morphisms for each $A in bar.v cal(C) bar.v$ such that each $d_A : cal(C) ( A   A )$ is idempotent, i.e., $d_A ; d_A = d_A$. We may then define the subsemicategory $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ of $cal(C)$ to be given by $ sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( A   B ) = { f in cal(C) ( A   B ) divides f = d_A ; f ; d_B } = { d_A ; f ; d_B divides f in cal(C) ( A   B ) } $ Note that $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ is #emph[not] a subcategory unless $d_A = sans("id")_A$ for all $A$, in which case $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) = cal(C)$. However, $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ can be #emph[independently] viewed as a category with identity $d_A$ at $A$.

]
We have that, if $cal(C)$ has coproducts and $d_(A + B) = d_A + d_B$, then $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ inherits coproducts from $cal(C)$ with injections $ d_A ; iota_l & = iota_l ; d_(A + B) & = d_A ; iota_l ; d_(A + B) & in sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( A   A + B )\
d_B ; iota_r & = iota_r ; d_(A + B) & = d_B ; iota_r ; d_(A + B) & in sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( B   A + B ) $ since, for $f in sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( A   C )$, $g in sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( A   C )$, we have that $ d_(A + B) ; \[ f   g \] ; d_C = d_A + B ; \[ f   g \] ; d_C = \[ d_A ; f ; d_C   d_B ; g ; d_C \] = \[ f   g \] $ In particular, it follows that $sans("Ide")_d$ preserves coproducts. If $cal(C)$ in addition has an Elgot structure, then $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ also inherits this structure (and therefore $sans("Ide")_d$ preserves it), as for $f in sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d ) ( A   B + A )$, we have $ f^dagger = ( d_A ; f ; d_B + d_A )^dagger = ( d_A ; f ; d_B + d_A )^dagger ; d_B = d_A ; ( f ; d_B + ( d_A ; d_A ) )^dagger ; d_B = d_A ; f^dagger ; d_B $ On the other hand, if $cal(C)$ is equipped with a (symmetric) premonoidal structure and we have that $ d_(A ⊗ B) = d_A times.l d_B = d_A times.r d_B #h(2em) d_(A ⊗ I) = d_A ⊗ I #h(2em) d_(I ⊗ A) = I ⊗ d_A $<eqn:d-premonoidal> (note that $d$ does #emph[not] have to be central!), it follows that $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ has (symmetric) premonoidal structure as well, with inherited tensor product functors and associators, unitors, and symmetries given by $ d_(( A ⊗ B ) ⊗ C) ; alpha_(A   B   C) = alpha_(A   B   C) ; d_(A ⊗ ( B ⊗ C )) = d_(( A ⊗ B ) ⊗ C) ; alpha_(A   B   C) ; d_(A ⊗ ( B ⊗ C ))\
d_(A ⊗ I) ; lambda_A = lambda_A ; d_A = d_(A ⊗ I) ; lambda_A ; d_A #h(2em) d_(I ⊗ A) ; rho_A = rho_A ; d_A = d_(I ⊗ A) ; rho_A ; d_A\
d_(A ⊗ B) ; sigma_(A   B) = sigma_(A   B) ; d_(B ⊗ A) = d_(A ⊗ B) ; sigma_(A   B) ; d_(B ⊗ A) $ respectively. It follows that if $cal(C)$ is distributive, so is $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$, since we can verify that the distributor $\[ d_A ⊗ ( d_B ; iota_l )   d_A ⊗ ( d_C ; iota_r ) \]$ has the expected inverse $ d_(A ⊗ ( B + C )) ; delta^(- 1) ; d_(( A ⊗ B ) + ( A ⊗ C )) $ If we in fact have that $d_(A ⊗ B) = A ⊗ d_B = d_A ⊗ B$ for all $A   B$ (which implies Equation~#todo[Cross-reference: \@eqn:d-premonoidal]) and $cal(C)$ is equipped with the structure of a Freyd category, then so is $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$, with projections and diagonal given by $ d_(A ⊗ B) ; pi_l = pi_l ; d_A = d_(A ⊗ B) ; pi_l ; d_A #h(2em) d_(A ⊗ B) ; pi_r = pi_r ; d_B = d_(A ⊗ B) ; pi_r ; d_B\
d_A ; Delta = Delta ; d_(A ⊗ A) = d_A ; Delta ; d_(A ⊗ A) $ Hence, it follows that if $cal(C)$ is an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ (expression) model, $d_A$ is idempotent, $d_(A + B) = d_A + d_B$, and $d_(A ⊗ B) = A ⊗ d_B = d_A ⊗ B$, then $sans("Ide") ( cal(C)   d )$ is an $lambda_(sans("SSA"))$ (expression) model as well. It turns out that all these properties hold for $d = sans("pflush")$, giving us our final categorical model for TSO weak memory, $sans("PTSO") = sans("Ide") ( sans("Set")_(sans("TSO"))   sans("pflush") )$.

== Brookes-Style Models
<apx:rel-acq>
In this section, we show how to construct a concurrency monad from a #cite(<brookes-full-abstraction-96>, form: "prose")-style model of concurrency, which we axiomatize. We will then explicitly show how to instantiate the model for sequentially consistent execution from #cite(<brookes-full-abstraction-96>, form: "prose"). We begin by defining a #emph[countable closure operator], given below:

#block[
We define a #emph[(countable) closure operator] $sans(c) : cal(P) ( T ) arrow.r cal(P) ( T )$ on $T$ to be a function on sets of $T$ which:

- #emph[is extensive]: $X subset.eq sans(c) ( X )$

- #emph[is idempotent]: $sans(c) ( sans(c) ( X ) ) = sans(c) ( X )$

- #emph[distributes over countable unions]: $sans(c) ( union.big_i X_i ) = union.big_i sans(c) ( X_i )$

We say a set $X$ such that $sans(c) ( X ) = X$ is #emph[closed] under $sans(c)$.

]
Note that this differs from the standard definition of a Kuratowski topological closure operator, which only requires us to distributive over #emph[finite] unions. We define the #emph[lift] of a closure operator $sans(c)_A : cal(P) ( T times A ) arrow.r cal(P) ( T times A )$ to be given by $ sans(c)_A ( X ) := union.big_(a in A) { ( t'   a ) divides t' in sans(c) ( { t divides ( t   a ) in X } ) } $ Identifying $cal(P) ( T times A )$ with $A arrow.r cal(P) ( T )$, this is equivalent to stating that $sans(c)_A ( X ) := sans(c) compose X$. It is hence easy to check that this gives a closure operator on $T times A$.

#block[
Given a monoid of #emph[traces] $T$ and a closure operator $sans(c) : cal(P) ( T ) arrow.r cal(P) ( T )$, we define the #emph[Brookes monad] $sans(B)_(sans(c))$ over $sans(c)$ to be given by closed sets of pairs $t therefore a$ of #emph[traces] $t in T$ and #emph[results] $a in A$. In particular, we define $ sans(B)_(sans(c)) ( A ) := sans(c)_A ( cal(P) ( T times A ) ) tilde.eq A arrow.r sans(c) ( cal(P) ( T ) ) $ with #todo[Native Typst port required for the following source equation, proof tree, or figure; exact LaTeX is preserved immediately below.]
```latex
\$\$\\eta\_{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{B}}\_{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{c}}}}\\;a := \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{c}}\_A(\\{1 \\therefore a\\}) \\qquad
  X \\mathbin{\>\\!\\!\>\\mkern-6.7mu=}\_{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{B}}\_{\\ensuremath{\\mathsf{c}}}} f 
  := \\ensuremath{\\mathsf{c}}\_A(\\{t \\cdot t\' \\therefore b \\mid \\exists a . t \\therefore a \\in X, t\' \\therefore b \\in X\'\\})\$\$
``` It is easy to see that the Brookes monad is in fact poset-enriched under the inclusion order. We say a Brookes monad is #emph[standard] if $T = ⟨ S   S ⟩^(\*)$ is the monoid of finite sequences of #emph[rely-guarantee pairs] of #emph[states] $S$.

]
We note that this definition gives us a Kleisli category which is not only poset-enriched, but in fact compatible with countable unions: with the usual definition $union_i f_i := lambda a . union_i f_i ( a )$, we have $ ( ( union.big_i f_i ) ; g ) ( a ) & := sans(c)_C ( { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in union.big_i f_i ( a )   t' therefore c in g ( b ) } )\
 & = sans(c) ( union.big_i { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in f_i ( a )   t' therefore c in g ( b ) } )\
 & = union.big_i sans(c) ( { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in f_i ( a )   t' therefore c in g ( b ) } )\
 & = union.big_i ( f_i ; g ) ( a )\
f ; ( union.big_i g_i ) ( a ) & := sans(c) ( { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in f ( a )   t' therefore c in union.big_i g_i ( b ) } )\
 & = sans(c) ( union.big_i { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in f ( a )   t' therefore c in g_i ( b ) } )\
 & = union.big_i sans(c) ( { t dot.op t' therefore c divides exists b . t therefore b in f ( a )   t' therefore c in g_i ( b ) } )\
 & = union.big_i ( f ; g_i ) ( a ) $ and likewise for coproducts.

We can always equip $sans(B)_(sans(c))$ with an Elgot structure $( dot.op )^dagger$ by, for $f : A arrow.r sans(B)_(sans(c)) ( B + A )$, defining $ f^dagger := union.big_(i in bb(N)) f_i $ where $f_i : A arrow.r sans(B)_(cal(C)) ( B )$ are the #emph[iterates] of $f$, defined inductively as follows $ f_0 := 0_(A   B) = ( lambda x . diameter ) #h(2em) f_(i + 1) := f ; \[ sans("id")_B   f_i \] $ We note in particular that $forall i . f_i subset.eq f_(i + 1)$; similarly, if $g subset.eq f$, we have that $g_i subset.eq f_i$. We can think of analysis using this structure as corresponding to #emph[partial correctness], since any infinite traces are simply discarded (a program which always diverges will have denotation $diameter$). It is straightforward to check that this is indeed an Elgot structure, since it satisfies:

- #emph[Fixpoint]: we have, since $f_0 union g = g$, $ f^dagger = union.big_(i in bb(N)) f_i = union.big_(i in bb(N)) f_(i + 1) = union.big_(i in bb(N)) ( f ; \[ sans("id")_B   f_i \] ) = f ; \[ sans("id")_B   union.big_(i in bb(N)) f_i \] = f ; \[ sans("id")_B   f^dagger \] $ as desired.

- #emph[Naturality]: by induction, we show that $( f ; g + A )_i = f_i ; g$, since $( f ; g + A )_0 = 0_(A   C) = 0_(A   B) ; g$ and $ ( f ; g + A )_(i + 1) = f ; g + A ; \[ sans("id")_C   ( f ; g + A )_i \] = f ; \[ g   f_i ; g \] = f ; \[ sans("id")_B   f_i \] ; g = f_(i + 1) ; g $ and therefore $ ( f ; g + A )^dagger = union.big_(i in bb(N)) ( f ; g + A )_i = union.big_(i in bb(N)) ( f_i ; g ) = ( union.big_(i in bb(N)) f_i ) ; g = f^dagger ; g $

- #emph[Codiagonal]: given $f : A arrow.r ( B + A ) + A$, define $g = f ; \[ sans("id")_(B + A)   iota_r \]$ and $h = f^dagger$. We have $ h_(i + 1) & = f^dagger ; \[ sans("id")   h_i \] = f ; \[ sans("id")   f^dagger \] ; \[ sans("id")   h_i \]\
   & = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   h_i \]   f^dagger ; \[ sans("id")   h_i \] \] = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   h_i \]   h_(i + 1) \]\
  g_(i + 1) & = f ; \[ sans("id")_B   iota_r \] ; \[ sans("id")   g_i \] = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g_i \]   g_i \] $ It follows by induction that $g_i subset.eq h_i$, since $g_0 = h_0$, and $ g_(i + 1) = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g_i \]   g_i \] subset.eq f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   h_i \]   h_i \] subset.eq f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   h_i \]   h_(i + 1) \] = h_(i + 1) $ We therefore have that $g^dagger subset.eq h^dagger$. On the other hand, we may show by induction on $j$ that $f_j ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] subset.eq g^dagger$, since $0 subset.eq g^dagger$ and $ f_(j + 1) ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] & = f ; \[ sans("id")   f_j \] ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \]   f_j ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] \] subset.eq f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \]   g^dagger \] = g^dagger $ We now show by induction that $h_i subset.eq g^dagger$: noting that $h_0 = 0 subset.eq g^dagger$, it suffices to show that $ h_(i + 1) & = f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   h_i \]   f^dagger ; \[ sans("id")   h_i \] \] subset.eq f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \]   f_j ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] \]\
   & = union.big_(j in bb(N)) ( f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \]   f_j ; \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \] \] ) subset.eq union.big_(j in bb(N)) ( f ; \[ \[ sans("id")   g^dagger \]   g^dagger \] \] ) = g^dagger $ It follows that $h^dagger subset.eq g^dagger$ and hence that $h^dagger = g^dagger$, as desired.

- #emph[Uniformity]: given arbitrary $f : A arrow.r B + A$, $g : X arrow.r B + X$ and $h : X arrow.r A$ such that $h ; f = g ; B + h$, for all $f_i$, we show by induction that $h ; f_i = g_i$ since $h ; f_0 = 0 = g_0$ and $ h ; f_(i + 1) = h ; f ; \[ sans("id")   f_i \] = g ; B + h ; \[ sans("id")   f_i \] = g ; \[ sans("id")   h ; f_i \] = g ; \[ sans("id")   g_i \] = g_(i + 1) $ Therefore, $h ; f^dagger = union.big_i h ; f_i = union.big_i g_i = g^dagger$, as desired.

Following #cite(<brookes-full-abstraction-96>, form: "prose"), we can build up a model of #emph[sequentially consistent] concurrent computation by taking a standard Brookes monad with

- States maps from locations to values $S := sans("Loc") arrow.r sans("Val")$

- Closure operator generated by:

  - #emph[Stuttering] $forall mu in S . dot.op arrow.r.twohead ⟨ mu   mu ⟩$

  - #emph[Mumbling] $forall mu   rho   theta in S . ⟨ mu   rho ⟩ ⟨ rho   theta ⟩ arrow.r.twohead ⟨ mu   theta ⟩$

The semantics of a write, for example, is then given by $ sans("write") : sans("Loc") times sans("Val") arrow.r sans(B)_(sans(c)) ( upright(bold(1)) ) := lambda ( ell   v ) . sans(c)_(upright(bold(1))) ( { ⟨ mu   \[ ell mapsto v \] mu ⟩ therefore ( ) divides mu in S } ) $ More complex states (e.g. involving per-thread buffers) and closure operators can allow us to model weak memory models. In particular, #cite(<jagadeesan-brookes-relaxed-12>, form: "prose") gives a Brookes model of TSO, while #cite(<release-acquire>, form: "prose") gives a Brookes model of release-acquire.

== Non-examples
<non-examples>
Weak memory is very complicated, and it has been very difficult to build models which satisfy even "obvious" properties. For example, the model of #cite(<jagadeesan-pwp-20>, form: "prose") fails to even satisfy the associativity of sequencing (i.e., $⟦ ( S_1 ; S_2 ) ; S_3 ⟧ = ⟦ S_1 ; ( S_2 ; S_3 ) ⟧$), and it took a nontrivial effort by #cite(<leaky-semicolon>, form: "prose") to establish this property. However, properties like distributivity $ ⟦ S ; sans("if") #h(0em) b #h(0em) { T_1 } #h(0em) sans("else") #h(0em) { T_2 } ⟧ = ⟦ sans("if") #h(0em) b #h(0em) { S ; T_1 } #h(0em) sans("else") #h(0em) { S ; T_2 } ⟧ $ do not hold, and there is often no semantics of loops at all. This makes them impossible to apply to SSA without extensive adaptation.

One of the easiest ways to build a model with "nice" properties is to make the model monadic; indeed, all the models presented in the above section are an example of this technique. This handles sequencing and distributivity "for free" via the monad laws and the fact that monads always preserve coproducts; however, this still requires us to verify the Conway iteration axioms to support loops, as is done in #cite(<xia-itrees-20>, form: "prose"). Our work identifies the minimal conditions required to have a compositional model of general #emph[first-order] side effects compatible with the standard interpretation of SSA; the higher-order analogue is precisely a (complete) Elgot monad @adamek-elgot-11, as presented in #cite(<goncharov-unguarded-18>, form: "prose").
